==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|la|(s)tewp}}
From {{der|la|ine-pro|*(s)tewp-}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*(s)tew-||to push, hit}}. Cognates include {{cog|grc|τύπτω||I strike}}, {{cog|sa|sc=Deva|तोपति|tr=tópati||to hurt}}, {{cog|sq|shtyj||to thrust}}, {{cog|cu|тъпати|tr=tŭpati}}, and {{cog|ang|styntan}} ({{cog|en|stint}}), Old English {{m|ang|stybb}} ({{cog|en|stub}}), Old English {{m|ang|stēap}} ({{cog|en|steep}})

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|stupeō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|stupeō|stupēre|stupuī|stupītum|conj=2|pattern=nopass}}

# {{lb|la|intransitive}} I am [[stunned]], [[stiffened]] or [[benumbed]], [[stop]], [[hesitate]].
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} I am [[dazed]], [[speechless]] or [[silenced]]; I am [[astounded]], [[confounded]], [[aghast]] or [[amazed]].
#* '''c. 50 {{C.E.}}''', [[w:Seneca the Younger|Seneca the Younger]], ''[[w:Phaedra (Seneca)|Phaedra]], 607
#*: Curae leues locuntur, ingentes '''stupent'''.
#*:: Trivial concerns talk, great ones are '''speechless'''.
# {{lb|la|transitive}} I am [[astonished]] or [[amazed]] at, [[wonder]] at.

